    "result_text": "Invoices now go from draft to sent to paid. Sending one writes a record, and you can only take payment on a sent invoice. `bin/e2e` builds the app and both Playwright tests pass.\n\n- **Stages:** new invoices start as `DRAFT`. On a project's invoice list, a draft shows a **Send** button. Once it's sent, it shows **Mark paid** instead, and a paid invoice shows no button.\n- **Record of sending:** each send writes a line like `INVOICE_SENT id=1 amount=100.00` to the audit log, the same way payments already did.\n- **Payment rule:** the server enforces it as well as the screen. Trying to send something that isn't a draft, or pay something that isn't sent, gets an error back (HTTP 409).\n- **Database:** a new migration, `V7__invoice_lifecycle.sql`, adds the three stages. Invoices already marked unpaid become `SENT`, because they had already gone out.\n- **Code:** the backend adds a `POST \u2026/invoices/{id}/send` endpoint alongside the existing pay one. The invoice list in `project-invoices.ts` has the new button and its own error message (`invoice-send-error`). No existing `data-testid`s were renamed or removed.\n\nThree things behave differently from before:\n- **Dashboard total:** \"outstanding\" now counts only sent, unpaid invoices. Drafts aren't owed yet, so they're left out.\n- **Test data:** an invoice seeded with no status now starts as `DRAFT` (it used to be treated as unpaid). Older fixtures that use `UNPAID` are loaded as `SENT`.\n- **Dates:** sending an invoice doesn't change its issued date. It keeps the date it was created with, or the one you entered.",
